Method overloading in Java is a feature that allows a class to have multiple methods with the same name but different parameters. This means that different methods can perform the same task with different inputs, which can improve the flexibility and reusability of the code.

Here's a simple example to illustrate method overloading in Java:

```java
public class Calculator {
    // Method overloading with no parameters
    public void add() {
        System.out.println("Adding two numbers.");
    }

    // Method overloading with one parameter
    public void add(int a) {
        System.out.println("Adding one number: " + a);
    }

    // Method overloading with two parameters
    public void add(int a, int b) {
        System.out.println("Adding two numbers: " + (a + b));
    }

    // Method overloading with three parameters
    public void add(int a, int b, int c) {
        System.out.println("Adding three numbers: " + (a + b + c));
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Calculator calc = new Calculator();
        calc.add(); // Calling the method with no parameters
        calc.add(5); // Calling the method with one parameter
        calc.add(3, 4); // Calling the method with two parameters
        calc.add(1, 2, 3); // Calling the method with three parameters
    }
}
```

In this example, the `add` method in the `Calculator` class is overloaded to handle different numbers of arguments. When the `add` method is called without any arguments, it prints a message indicating that two numbers are being added. When called with one integer argument, it prints the number being added. When called with two integer arguments, it prints the sum of the two numbers. Similarly, when called with three integer arguments, it prints the sum of the three numbers.

This method overloading is a powerful feature in Java that allows for more flexible and readable code. It allows developers to perform the same operation using different inputs, making the code more modular and easier to maintain.

