System.out.println(str4.equals(str6)); // prints false because str4 and str6 contain different content
```

In summary, use `==` when you want to compare the references of objects and `equals()` when you want to compare the contents of objects. It's important to understand when and how to use each method to correctly perform object comparisons in Java.

